// NEGATIVE TEST — both endpoints of a range pattern are the same kind of literal.
//
// `char` joined the integer as an ordered literal kind when char patterns landed,
// which creates a shape that could not exist while there was only one: a range
// with ONE END OF EACH KIND. It is refused by name rather than compared, because
// the only way to order a scalar against an integer is the implicit conversion
// N4-04 exists to forbid — `char` is DISTINCT from `i64` in both directions, so
// `'a'..=9` has no meaning to fall back on.
//
// A SEPARATE FIXTURE from the string-endpoint one: that refuses a kind this
// language does not order at all, this refuses two kinds it orders separately,
// and the two diagnostics come from different branches of the same rule.
fn main() {
    let c: char = 'a';
    let v = match c {
        'a'..=9 => 1,
        _ => 0,
    };
    print_int(v);
}
